145:  ["2013-2025", "SLSA Lifesaver of the Year Panel; led the 2021 review of lifesaving award criteria and introduced the Youth Surf Life Saver of the Year award."],
146:  ["2016-2025", "National Chair, Lifesaving (SLSA)."],
189:  "Chris has spent twenty years advancing lifesaving nationally, 14 on the Lifesaving Members Advisory Committee and 9 as National Chair. He reshaped how every Australian lifesaver trains through the curriculum review, PSAR manual rewrite and three course redesigns. He then looked ahead, founding National Innovation Day, leading Lifesaving CPR's rollout and chairing the Expanding Horizons Review.";
195:  "Chris's state service is distinguished by both breadth and depth, taking his work across states. He ran SLST's most successful lifesaving development programme for six years (2014-2020), then started the same programme from scratch in Queensland (2019) and NSW (2021-22), carrying it personally into each state. As Chair of Lifesaving (SLST) before he stepped up to the national chair, he authored Tasmania's Duty Officer training and standards and authored SLSTs inaugural Standard Operating Procedures. He still serves Tasmania today on the SLST Operations Executive (2025-present), even after moving to NSW. Being named Volunteer, Lifesaver and Trainer of the Year marks service his peers judged the best in the state across multiple domains, and it has been sustained across more than two decades and three member states. Chris continues to be a key part of initiatives across multiple states across the country.";
197:  "Chris is known across SLSA for moving the organisation forward. He did not just lead the Lifesaving portfolio, he pushed it into the future, driving innovation, challenging the status quo and lifting standards. His national contribution began 20 years ago at the 2006 Youth Development Forum and has continued without pause, with 14 years on the Lifesaving Members Advisory Committee, 9 as National Chair. He chaired the National Lifesaving Curriculum Review, the PSAR manual rewrite and three course redesigns, reshaping how every Australian lifesaver is trained, and built the first nationally delivered blended learning model. He founded National Innovation Day, was National Implementation Lead for Lifesaving CPR, an Australian first, and founded and chaired the Expanding Horizons Review. His efforts and initiatives have earned the praise of even his harshest critics, and his commitment and passion are so well known that members often mistake him for paid staff, yet he remains a volunteer.";
201:  "The Lifesaving CPR Team designed, built and nationally delivered Lifesaving CPR, an evidence-based, standardised and simplified approach to resuscitation, delivered as the first nationally coordinated CPR quality improvement initiative implemented by a community first aid organisation in Australia. Drawing on the team's long-running expertise in the SLSA Medical Advisory Group and CPR curriculum, it translates High Performance CPR and ANZCOR guidelines into six clear Aims, a national online learning package, a skills-maintenance programme and standardised multi-operator protocols, rolled out to every club across the country over a two-year period. Comparable organisations with fewer volunteers judged a rollout of this size, and a change this significant, to be a multi-year undertaking or impossible; SLSA delivered it, and the rollout feedback was highly positive on content, engagement and process. It is a genuine first, executed at national scale, that has placed Australian surf lifesaving on the global stage in resuscitation. This is legacy work, built by our movement's senior resuscitation advisors, designed to improve survival from cardiac arrest on and off the beach.";

